/** `spec/m/meta.tn`'s own header: `!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=c9b069b606f7cc8a043ebabe3fe91e4aaa35a9ffd814b68be0ba4bbe6d02bfb5"` */
export const META_TN =
  '!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=c9b069b606f7cc8a043ebabe3fe91e4aaa35a9ffd814b68be0ba4bbe6d02bfb5"\n@doc:"""\n  TSON meta — the pre-loaded meta kernel (2026 Revision 39 draft).\n\n  The kernel holds the series\' structural vocabulary: the one wait cycle a\n  processor closes by pre-loading, the one holding `meta.record` ([TSON-SCHEMA]\n  §3.4), every name its declarations reference, so it resolves and links on\n  its own, and the concepts that belong with them — the map, tuple and choice\n  constructors, held template bodies, the `meta.ref<T>` reference, the\n  one-element tuple templates, the octet-sequence constructor, and the\n  classifying annotations the data-format libraries state their types with\n  (`atom.ordering`, `atom.bounded`, `atom.exact`, `atom.numeric`).\n\n  Its root holds the vocabulary an author writes: `void`, `boolean`,\n  `integer`, `int64`, `text`, `bytes`, `iri`, `iri_reference`, `regex`,\n  `doc`, the `enum` constructor, the `set<T>` template and `schema`, the tag\n  of a schema map. Everything else is machinery, a member of one of six\n  schema maps nested in this document\'s root, each member named by its path\n  from the root:\n\n    meta      the type system: the kinds and constructors, fields,\n              collections, references, templates, aliases, `meta.schema`,\n              the name families (`meta.identifier`, `meta.type_name`,\n              `meta.field_name`, `meta.param_name`), `meta.disjoint` and the\n              one-element tuples\n    atom      the datatype properties of ISO/IEC 11404 (`@atom.ordering`,\n              `@atom.bounded`, `@atom.exact`, `@atom.numeric`) and the\n              `atom.specification` mixin\n    numeric   the integer constructor `numeric.integer_type`, its facet\n              vocabulary, and `numeric.count`\n    unicode   the text constructors `unicode.text_type`,\n              `unicode.identifier_type` and `unicode.regex_type`, and their\n              facet vocabulary\n    net       the IRI constructor `net.iri_type` and its schemes\n    binary    the octet-sequence constructor `binary.bytes_type` and its\n              encodings\n\n  A schema whose import closure holds this document reserves those six map\n  names and the thirteen root names ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3). Every name resolves\n  from the root, so an entry here naming a member of a map writes its path,\n  as an importer does.\n\n  Every map is open: any schema may add a member to it. A schema map declared\n  by several documents of one closure is the union of their members, and a\n  member path two of them declare collides as any name does ([TSON-SCHEMA]\n  §2.2.3).\n\n  The kernel imports nothing and is never resolved from a document: it is\n  pre-loaded by special resolution ([TSON-SCHEMA] §3.4), and must be the\n  revision the processor was built for. Its root tag and the tag of each map\n  it nests, `!schema`, name its own `schema`, an alias of `meta.schema`, as\n  every schema document\'s root tag does. Implementations ship the kernel\'s\n  resolved structure, and this document describes it.\n\n  The data-format libraries build on it, each importing this document alone\n  and declaring its constructors under the map named for it, numeric.tn and\n  net.tn adding to this document\'s `numeric` and `net`, and its instances at\n  its root: numeric.tn (`number`, `int32`, `float64`, `numeric.float_type`),\n  temporal.tn (`date`, `datetime`, `duration`, `temporal.date_type`) and\n  net.tn (`uuid`, `uri`, `ipv4`, `net.uuid_type`). core.tn imports the three,\n  so a schema importing it has every bundled type, and declares the author\n  annotations (`@title`, `@deprecated`, …). policy.tn declares the processor\n  policy\'s vocabulary.\n\n  Hash pins are real digests: sha256 over every byte of a document past its\n  `!!id` line ([TSON-DATA] §2.2.1), which is what lets an `!!id` carry the\n  digest of the document it identifies. Each library pins this file and\n  core.tn pins the libraries, so an edit here moves every pin above it.\n\n  References:\n    ISO/IEC 11404:2007    General Purpose Datatypes (the classifying\n                          annotations)\n    RFC 3987              Internationalized Resource Identifiers (IRIs)\n    RFC 4648              Base encodings (text spellings of `bytes`)\n    RFC 9485              I-Regexp, the interoperable regular expression\n    CommonMark 0.31.2     The text of `doc`\n  """\n!schema {\n\n  numeric => !schema {\n    @doc:"""\n      Fixed-width integer representation: a bit width paired with its\n      two\'s-complement signedness. A width above zero is a schema-load\n      coherence check: zero or a negative `bits` is a schema-load error.\n      """\n    integer_size => {\n      bits:    integer\n      signed:  boolean\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The members of an integer_type. Member identity is [TSON-DATA] §4.3\'s, so\n      80 and 0x50 are one member and a duplicate rather than two.\n      """\n    integer_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: integer  min_items: 1 }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Integer constraint vocabulary. Atom constructor whose instance,\n      `integer`, is the arbitrary-precision integer. Its field types name\n      `integer`, the constructor\'s own instance; a name at a type position\n      orders nothing, so the mutual reference closes. Both reach every schema\n      whose import closure includes this document.\n\n      Bounds: each side is a field group ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.11) — the inclusive\n      `min` or the exclusive `exclusive_min`; likewise `max` or `exclusive_max`\n      — so at most one bound per side holds by shape. That the lower bound not\n      exceed the upper (across whichever forms are present) is a schema-load\n      coherence check.\n\n      Fixed width: `size` gives a fixed representation width; when absent the\n      integer is unbounded arbitrary precision (the base `integer`). Width and\n      signedness derive the representable range — signed n-bit is [-2^(n-1),\n      2^(n-1)-1], unsigned n-bit is [0, 2^n-1] — so `size` and the bound groups\n      are equivalent ways to state a bound. The two compose: bounds and\n      `multiple_of` narrow within the width-derived range, and a bound outside\n      that range is a resolver error. Signedness is two\'s complement. `size`\n      names the target representation a consumer decodes into, not the source\n      form. Resolver output stores `size` as written and does not expand it to\n      bounds.\n\n      Step: `multiple_of` is strictly positive; zero or a negative value is a\n      schema-load error. The test ignores the sign of the value, so -30 is a\n      multiple of 15. A refinement may only tighten `multiple_of` to an integer\n      multiple of the inherited value (15 under 5 is a tightening; 10 under 15\n      is an error). It composes with every other facet present: a value must\n      satisfy all of them.\n\n      Enumeration: `members` lists the admitted values outright — the spelling\n      for numeric codes, an enum\'s members being text\n      (`!integer ^ { members: [1 2 3] }`, not `!enum [1 2 3]`). Every\n      member must itself satisfy the bounds, the width and `multiple_of` present\n      on the same body — a member outside them is a schema-load error — and a\n      refinement may only shrink the set (the member-set rule of the tightening\n      table). A value must be a member and satisfy the other facets; with a\n      coherent set the second test is implied by the first.\n      """\n    integer_type => meta.atom & {\n      size?: numeric.integer_size\n      ( min: integer | exclusive_min: integer )?\n      ( max: integer | exclusive_max: integer )?\n      multiple_of?: integer\n      members?:     numeric.integer_member_set\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The type of every facet that counts something: lengths, item counts,\n      digit counts, prefix lengths, fractional-second precision. It is the\n      non-negative half of `int64`, 0 to 2^63-1, so every implementation holds\n      a count in a native type (JavaScript in a BigInt).\n      """\n    count => !int64 ^ { min: 0 }\n  }\n\n  unicode => !schema {\n    @doc:"""\n      The members of a text_type. The set\'s own uniqueness compares the text as\n      written; each member is then put into the constrained type\'s normalization\n      form, and two that are one value there are a duplicate, as text_type says.\n      """\n    text_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: text  min_items: 1 }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Text constraint vocabulary. Atom constructor whose instance, `text`, is\n      the kernel\'s Unicode code point sequence type. Lengths count code points.\n\n      `members` lists the admitted strings outright. Every member must satisfy\n      the other facets on the same body, the pattern included.\n\n      `normalization` is the form a value is put into. A value is its token\'s\n      text, unquoted, unescaped and then put into that form; every other facet\n      judges the value and never the spelling, and so does every comparison of\n      two values. A member is a value too, and two members that are one value\n      are refused.\n\n      `pattern` and `members` are each settable once: a refinement may set one\n      the source left unset, or restate the source\'s own, and never change it.\n      `normalization` is fixed where the type is constructed: a refinement\n      restates it or leaves it, since changing it would change what a token\n      means rather than narrow the values admitted.\n      """\n    text_type => meta.atom & {\n      min_length?:    numeric.count\n      max_length?:    numeric.count\n      length?:        numeric.count\n      pattern?:       regex\n      members?:       unicode.text_member_set\n      normalization?: unicode.normalization ~ NONE\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The base a UAX #31 Start or Continue set is drawn from: XID_Start /\n      XID_Continue, ID_Start / ID_Continue, their ASCII members (Start A-Z a-z,\n      Continue adding 0-9 and "_"), or the empty set.\n      """\n    identifier_base => !enum [XID ID ASCII NONE]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The form a text value is put into. NONE keeps the text as written. NFC and\n      NFKC are Unicode\'s normalization forms. NFKC_CASEFOLD is toNFKC_Casefold,\n      UAX #31\'s form for names compared without case: over ASCII it lowercases,\n      so Content-Type and content-type are one value. ASCII_CASEFOLD maps A-Z to\n      a-z and changes nothing else, the comparison of case-insensitive ASCII\n      names such as URI schemes and HTTP field names.\n      """\n    normalization => !enum [NONE NFC NFKC NFKC_CASEFOLD ASCII_CASEFOLD]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Identifier constraint vocabulary: a UAX #31 identifier profile, with the\n      text facets applied inside it. It composes text_type\'s record shape, so it\n      takes lengths, a pattern such as a naming convention, and members for a\n      closed vocabulary of names. The profile is UAX #31\'s R1 default identifier\n      syntax:\n\n        meta.identifier := Start Continue* (Medial Continue+)*\n        Start      := (start ∪ start_add) − exclude\n        Continue   := (continue ∪ continue_add) − exclude\n        Medial     := medial\n\n      where start and continue name the base each set is drawn from, and each of\n      start_add, continue_add, medial and exclude is the set of code points its\n      text holds. A medial character stands only between two others and never\n      beside another medial, so medial must be disjoint from Start and Continue.\n      The profile judges the value, which is the text put into the normalization\n      form; the form defaults to NFC here. A join control (U+200C, U+200D) is\n      admitted only in the contexts UTS #39 §3.1.1.1 permits, whatever the\n      profile.\n\n      The profile facets are fixed where the profile is constructed: a\n      refinement restates each or leaves it, and narrows only the text facets. A\n      different profile is a fresh `!unicode.identifier_type`.\n\n      identifier is [TSON-DATA] §7.7\'s instance, and the type of every naming\n      position in the series — type names, field names and parameter names,\n      through the three roles below. A rule stated here reaches all of them. An\n      identifier is a name, not a lexeme: it is the decoded text of a token,\n      after unquoting, escape processing and normalisation, and it is\n      constrained however it was spelled. Its profile is stated beside the\n      unquoted-token profile of [TSON-DATA] §7.1:\n\n        Start    = XID_Start\n        Continue = XID_Continue + "-"\n\n      and its value is the text in NFC. The profile is the token profile minus\n      the extensions the number grammar requires, so an identifier never begins\n      with a digit or a sign, which subsumes the rule that numbers are not\n      declarable names. "+" is dropped from Continue, and "." is reserved as a\n      future identifier separator. Everything else follows from XID membership:\n      whitespace, control characters, emoji, unassigned code points and every\n      format character but the two join controls are none of them XID_Continue.\n\n      Every part of identifier\'s profile lies inside the unquoted-token profile,\n      so every identifier is a well-formed unquoted token and none ever needs\n      quoting. Another profile carries no such guarantee, and a value under it\n      may need quoting.\n\n      A schema that types a value or a map key as a name of another family\n      declares its own identifier_type instance under a name of its own, such as\n      `slug => !unicode.identifier_type { continue_add: "-" }`.\n      """\n    identifier_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:          = "https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr31/"\n      start?:         unicode.identifier_base ~ XID\n      continue?:      unicode.identifier_base ~ XID\n      start_add?:     text\n      continue_add?:  text\n      medial?:        text\n      exclude?:       text\n      normalization?: unicode.normalization ~ NFC\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Regex constraint vocabulary. Same shape pattern as iri_type, pinned to\n      I-Regexp. A regex is its text as written: putting it into another form\n      would change what it matches.\n      """\n    regex_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:          = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9485"\n      normalization?: = NONE\n    }\n  }\n\n  net => !schema {\n    @doc:"The schemes an iri_type or uri_type admits."\n    scheme_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: net.scheme_name  min_items: 1 }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A URI scheme (RFC 3986 §3.1): a letter, then letters, digits, "+", "-" and\n      ".". A scheme compares without case, so its value is the text folded.\n      """\n    scheme_name => !unicode.identifier_type { start: ASCII  continue: ASCII\n      continue_add: "+-."  exclude: "_"  normalization: ASCII_CASEFOLD }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      IRI constraint vocabulary per RFC 3987. Composes with the text_type\n      constructor to inherit its record shape (length and pattern fields) plus\n      the atom.specification mixin\'s spec field plus iri-specific fields.\n      `schemes` lists the admitted schemes as net.scheme_name values, which\n      fold case as RFC 3986 §3.1 compares a scheme, and a value\'s scheme is\n      compared in that form; a value with no scheme is outside it.\n      `allow_relative` admits a relative reference beside an IRI, so its default\n      is an IRI-reference; withdrawing it requires a scheme. `allow_fragment`\n      admits a fragment; withdrawing it refuses one. An IRI\'s characters beyond\n      US-ASCII are RFC 3987\'s `ucschar`, and `iprivate` within the query, and an\n      IRI is judged through the URI it maps to (RFC 3987 §3.1). `uri_type` is\n      the US-ASCII family with the same facets.\n\n      An IRI is its text as written: RFC 3986 says which of its parts compare\n      without case, and a form over the whole text would fold a path or a query\n      that is case-sensitive.\n      """\n    iri_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:           = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987"\n      schemes?:        net.scheme_set\n      allow_relative?: boolean ~ true\n      allow_fragment?: boolean ~ true\n      normalization?:  = NONE\n    }\n\n  }\n\n  atom => !schema {\n    @doc:"""\n      Mixin record contributing the `spec` field to specification-bound atoms.\n      """\n    specification => { spec: iri }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Ordering classification (ISO/IEC 11404 / XSD fundamental facet): whether\n      the value space has an order relation. Not a container\'s `ordered` facet,\n      which says whether element order is part of a value.\n      """\n    ordering => @meta.annotation !enum [NONE PARTIAL TOTAL]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Boundedness classification (ISO/IEC 11404 / XSD fundamental facet): true\n      when the value space has a finite least and greatest value.\n      """\n    bounded => @meta.annotation boolean\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Exactness classification (ISO/IEC 11404 characterizing property): true for\n      exact types (values preserved as written), false for approximate types\n      (values rounded to a representation grid).\n      """\n    exact => @meta.annotation boolean\n\n    @doc:"Numeric type marker annotation."\n    numeric => @meta.annotation void\n\n  }\n\n  @doc:"The type system, each member named by its path, `meta.record`."\n  meta => !schema {\n    @doc:"""\n      The structural root: every constructor IS-A `top`. Every constructor\n      transitively IS-A `atom`, `product` or `sum`, except `alias` and\n      `template`, which compose with `top` directly; `atom`, `product` and `sum`\n      IS-A `type`, which IS-A `top`. Construction transfers kind, not IS-A, so\n      instances and fresh records sit under the kinds with empty chains. `top`\n      and the kinds beneath it are abstract: none has instances of its own, so a\n      direct application of one is refused as any abstract record\'s is, naming\n      the subtypes that would satisfy it.\n\n      A schema map\'s entries are types or values (`schema`). An entry whose\n      value is built by a constructor IS-A `type` is a type; one whose value is\n      built by `alias` or `template` is an alias or an open entry; and one whose\n      value is an instance of an ordinary type, `orders => !api { ... }` or\n      `create_order => !operation { ... }`, is a value, marked by nothing but\n      its tag.\n      """\n    top       => abstract {}\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Where an entry sits in the IS-A order. `parents` is what was written: the\n      parents of a record template, one of which may be an application\n      (`<T> result<T> & { ... }`), kept on the template entry once they resolve;\n      a closed instantiation keeps none. It is the only list that ever holds an\n      application. `supertypes` is the closed chain: every entry this one IS-A,\n      complete to a fixed point through applied parents ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.8),\n      each a name without arguments, sorted, and empty for a fresh record, an\n      instance and a subtracted record alike. `subtypes` is the inverse, over\n      the entries of this map only ([TSON-SCHEMA] §3.3.4).\n      """\n    type_chain => {\n      parents?:    [meta.type_ref]\n      supertypes?: [meta.type_ref]\n      subtypes?:   [meta.type_ref]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The kind of kinds that describe the shape of a data value: `atom`,\n      `product` and `sum` IS-A `type`, and nothing else does. An entry whose\n      value is a body whose constructor IS-A `type` is a type; an `alias`\n      or a `template` is not, composing `top`, and an entry whose value is an\n      instance of an ordinary type is a value, never a type.\n\n      A type carries its IS-A chain. A declaration never writes `chain`, and a\n      constructor never declares a field of that name: the resolver computes the\n      chain from composition and refinement and the linker inverts it. On ingest\n      it is read as stated and never recomputed, because a flat body and its\n      chain cannot be recomputed from each other.\n      """\n    type      => abstract meta.top & {\n      chain?: meta.type_chain\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The kind of a scalar: a value written as one token, which its atom family\n      reads into a value.\n      """\n    atom      => abstract meta.type & {}\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The kind of a compound value of parts: a record, an array, a map or a\n      tuple. `access_pattern` says whether a part is reached by position or by\n      name, and `size_type` whether the number of parts is fixed by the type.\n      """\n    product   => abstract meta.type & {\n      access_pattern: meta.product_access_type\n      size_type:      meta.product_size_type\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The kind of a value that is one of several types: a choice or a scope.\n      """\n    sum       => abstract meta.type & {}\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Aliasing body — the entry `target` names: a type, or a constructor, since\n      an alias of a constructor is applied as its target is ([TSON-SCHEMA]\n      §3.3.1), a constructor\'s value being a record. A partial application\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10, `uuid_pair => <B> pair<uuid, B>`) is an open entry,\n      and its target is the reference as written, inside the\n      `template.template` text: `!meta.alias { target: pair<uuid B> }`.\n\n      An alias is a hop, not a rewrite: resolved output states the chain as the\n      author wrote it, and a use site names whatever entry it names. A processor\n      collapses the chain when it compiles readers, after linking, once per\n      entry. A walk stops at an argument-bearing target, which is an application\n      rather than a further hop.\n      """\n    alias => meta.top & {\n      target: meta.type_ref\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      One parameter of an open entry: its name, and the type an argument for it\n      is read as — the declared type of the slot it stands in, in the vocabulary\n      of the constructor the held body applies. It is derived from the body and\n      never required of the author: a type slot gives `meta.type_ref`; a scalar\n      slot of the applied constructor gives that slot\'s declared type\n      (`min_items: N` gives `numeric.count`); a routed default or\n      fixed value gives the field\'s own declared type (`w?: int32 ~ N` gives\n      `int32`); and a parameter passed to another template takes that template\'s\n      recorded type for the position, with `meta.type_ref` where nothing\n      grounds it. A parameter is a type parameter exactly when its `type` is\n      `meta.type_ref`. A `type` may name an earlier parameter of the same\n      template, where a routed default\'s field is typed by one.\n\n      A declaration may narrow what it derives by writing a type after the name,\n      `<T: text, N: int8>`, read by the kind the positions give. On a value\n      parameter it narrows `type` itself and must IS-A the derived type. On a\n      type parameter it is `bound`: an argument must name a type that IS-A it.\n      `bound` is present only where `type` is `meta.type_ref`, and a parameter\n      passed to another template inherits that template\'s bound for the\n      position, so an unannotated parameter is bounded as narrowly as its uses\n      require.\n      """\n    template_param => {\n      name:   meta.param_name\n      type:   meta.type_ref\n      bound?: meta.type_ref\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Held body — the body of an entry that declares type parameters and applies\n      any constructor but `record` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10), a record template\n      being a `record_template`. `template` is the constructor\n      application as written, unread until materialisation substitutes the\n      parameters away, and `parameters` lists what it binds, each a\n      `meta.template_param`.\n\n      The text is how a held body is written in resolved output; a processor\n      holds the parsed form, which this vocabulary cannot represent. A held body\n      is not a value of this vocabulary: a parameter stands wherever a token\n      stands — `min_items: N` as readily as `element_type: T` — so a body\n      carrying one is not typed by any constructor\'s own record shape until it\n      closes.\n\n      An open entry\'s value is this, built by a constructor IS-A `top`. What is\n      compared is the parsed form and never the text: an open\n      synthetic\'s identity is derived from its held binding record, so two\n      spellings of one form must reduce to one entry, and whitespace is free.\n\n      Composes `top` alone: it is not a type, its open form denotes nothing, and\n      naming it at a type position is an error. An application is its expansion,\n      and carries whatever chain the expansion\'s own `^` or `&` gives it.\n      """\n    template => meta.top & {\n      parameters: [meta.template_param]\n      template:   text\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Void primitive. The instance of void_type, an atom constructor with no\n      constraint vocabulary whose parsing contract admits only the void sentinel\n      `_`. It binds no host value. void is the target type for bare annotations\n      (`@T` with no value); the resolver fills the implicit `_` and validates\n      against this contract. See [TSON-SCHEMA] §6.\n      """\n    void_type => meta.atom & {}\n\n    @doc:"The default identifier type used for types/fields/params"\n    identifier => !unicode.identifier_type { continue_add: "-" }\n\n    @doc:"A type\'s name: a schema-map key, and a segment of a reference."\n    type_name  => meta.identifier\n\n    @doc:"A field\'s name, in a record or a field group."\n    field_name => meta.identifier\n\n    @doc:"A template parameter\'s name."\n    param_name => meta.identifier\n\n    @doc:"How a product\'s parts are reached: by position, or by name."\n    product_access_type => !enum [INDEX NAMED]\n\n    @doc:"Whether a product\'s number of parts is fixed by its type."\n    product_size_type   => !enum [FIXED VARIABLE]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      What a record field\'s `value` is for: FREE has none, DEFAULT is injected\n      when the field is omitted and may be overridden, and FIXED is the only\n      value the field admits.\n      """\n    field_role          => !enum [FREE DEFAULT FIXED]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      How a record may be realised ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2). OPEN is the ordinary\n      case and the default: the record has instances of its own and may be\n      composed onto or refined. ABSTRACT has no instances of its own, so a value\n      at a position typed by it is a value of some subtype. FINAL has instances\n      and admits no subtype: no composition or refinement may name it as a\n      source. Subtraction stays admissible against a FINAL record — a subtracted\n      record\'s chain is empty, so it mints no IS-A edge, which is the only thing\n      FINAL constrains.\n\n      **How a subtype is selected is no part of this enum.** An ABSTRACT record\n      naming no `discriminators` is selected by the tag; one naming them is\n      selected by reading those fields.\n\n      Extensibility is never inherited. A subtype of an ABSTRACT record is OPEN\n      unless it says otherwise.\n\n      **A field written `=?` implies ABSTRACT.** Writing `abstract` beside a\n      selector asserts what the body already says and is admitted; `final`\n      beside one is refused.\n\n      **A family is open across schemas.** [TSON-SCHEMA] §3.3.4 makes `subtypes`\n      open, so an importing schema may declare a further member. A host\n      language\'s own closed-set construct, generated from a family, is therefore\n      relative to one closure and not a promise the schema makes.\n\n      The member is written as a word at the declaration —\n      `pet => abstract { … }`, [TSON-SCHEMA] §12.1 — and OPEN is the absence of\n      one.\n      """\n    record_extension_type => !enum [ABSTRACT FINAL OPEN]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The annotation type marker: a type carrying it is meant as annotation\n      metadata ([TSON-SCHEMA] §6).\n      """\n    annotation => void\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Resolver-attached and derived: discarded and recomputed on ingest\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §6, §8.1), and marks a resolver-materialised synthetic\n      entry at its schema-map key (§8.2).\n      """\n    synthetic => @meta.annotation void\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Resolver-attached and derived: on an entry\'s schema-map key, the\n      application the entry is the instantiation of ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2). An\n      entry of any kind can be one, so it is a fact about the entry and not\n      about a type: the resolver writes it wherever an entry closes an\n      application — a record template\'s member, an instance closed from a\n      templated construction, the alias a template\'s application closes to. On\n      ingest it is read as stated, unlike `synthetic`, which is recomputed: it\n      is the one fact the entry\'s value does not state, and what lets an\n      imported schema\'s instantiations unify with a later schema\'s. A\n      declaration never writes it, as it never writes `chain`. Its value is the\n      application as the reference written, nested arguments unreduced,\n      `@meta.application:foo<bar<text>>`.\n      """\n    application => @meta.annotation meta.type_ref\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Supporting records. A record field is the four facts a reader consults:\n      `optional`, that the key may be missing; `voidable`, that the value may be\n      void, the void sentinel `_` written in its place; `role`, what `value` is\n      for; and `value`, a concrete default or fixed value read against the\n      field\'s declared type — for a `meta.type_ref` slot, a type name. `value`\n      is typed `type!`, a dependent field: each record field\'s `value` is read\n      against the type its own `type` names. Its name\n      is not among them: a field is a value of `record.fields`, keyed by its\n      name. Each fact is one mark of the spelling `name?: type? ~ value`: the\n      name\'s `?`, the type\'s `?`, and `~` or `=` with its value. What omission\n      yields is derived, never stored: a missing field that is not optional is\n      an error, and a missing optional one with a value has it injected — except\n      at a field group\'s member, whose presence is the group\'s and which is\n      never supplied.\n\n      A record field is closed: its `type` names a type or a sibling, and its\n      `value` is a literal, never a parameter. A field that may carry one is a\n      `meta.record_template_field`, which only a record template holds.\n\n      A field whose `type` is a sibling reference, `kind!`, is a dependent\n      field ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2): each instance\'s value is read against the\n      type that instance\'s `kind` names. The sibling is declared before it, is\n      typed by a reference whose `of` lies within `meta.type`, is never\n      voidable, and is there to read whenever the dependent field is: required,\n      defaulted, pinned, or a member of a field group with the dependent field\n      optional, a group\'s own member counting as optional. Its default or pin\n      names a type a value can be read against. A member is absent when another\n      option is chosen, and a dependent field written without its sibling is\n      refused where it is read. A dependent field is never voidable, carries no\n      value of its own, and is restated only as itself; pinning its sibling\n      fixes its type.\n\n      Which fields a member-dispatched family dispatches on is the enclosing\n      record\'s own statement, `record.discriminators`, and no field says it of\n      itself. A field it names is required and not voidable, no member of a\n      field group, typed by an atom or an enum, and carries no value of its own;\n      each subtype restates it FIXED, the pins pairwise distinct under the field\n      type\'s own equality.\n\n      The two facts are the series\' two kinds of nothing, and each has one name\n      wherever it applies: a slot that may be missing is `optional`, on a record\n      field and a field group; a slot whose value may be void is `voidable`, on\n      a record field, an array\'s element, a map\'s value and a tuple position. A\n      container\'s part is never missing, so a container has no `optional`.\n      """\n    record_field => {\n      type:      meta.field_type_ref\n      optional?: boolean ~ false\n      voidable?: boolean ~ false\n      role?:     meta.field_role ~ FREE\n      value?:    type!\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Field group — a presence rule over fields of a record ([TSON-SCHEMA]\n      §5.11). `members` lists the group\'s options in source order, each the\n      fields it holds; a group has at least one option, and an option at least\n      one field. Every member is declared in the enclosing record\'s `fields`,\n      `optional` there and carrying no value, and sits in one option of one\n      group. An option is chosen when any of its members is present.\n\n      `optional_members` names the members that may be missing once their option\n      is chosen, and never the only member of an option; a chosen option holds\n      every member it does not name. `optional` says whether the group may be\n      missing as a whole, as an optional field\'s key may: without it exactly one\n      option is chosen, with it at most one. A group of one option is either not\n      optional with every member named in `optional_members`, so at least one is\n      present, or optional with at least two members and one not named. The\n      flattened fields-plus-groups encoding is canonical.\n      """\n    field_group => {\n      members:           [[text; 1..]; 1..]\n      optional_members?: [text; 1..]\n      optional?:         boolean ~ false\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The fields of a record ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2): each field\'s name mapped to\n      its facts, in declaration order, which is part of the value. The map\'s own\n      key uniqueness compares the text as written; each key is then read as a\n      value of the record\'s `name_type`, and two that are one value under that\n      type\'s equality are a duplicate, a resolver error. A field name is text\n      within its own record and is no namespace key: no qualified name selects\n      a field declaration.\n      """\n    field_map => !meta.map { key_type: text  value_type: meta.record_field  ordered: true }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A field of a record template: a record field\'s four facts, where the type\n      and the value may each be a parameter of the enclosing template, and the\n      label says which. `type_param` names a parameter standing for the whole\n      type, `value_param` one standing for the whole value; `type` and `value`\n      are as on a record field. A parameter may also stand as an argument of an\n      application in `type`, `list<T>`, recognised there because its text\n      resolves into the template\'s `parameters` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10\'s\n      shadowing rule). An application substitutes every parameter away, and\n      each of its fields is then a `meta.record_field`: `type_param` becomes\n      `type` and `value_param` becomes `value`, carrying the argument, and no\n      other fact changes.\n\n      A literal `value` needs a closed type, so a field whose type is a\n      parameter, or an application over one, takes `value_param` or nothing.\n      A field of a closed type takes either. `value` is typed `type!`, as on a\n      record field: beside `type_param`, with `type` absent, it has no type to\n      be read against and is refused where it is read.\n\n      A parametric modifier takes the facts its literal spelling takes\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.7): `w?: T ~ P` is an optional DEFAULT field and\n      `w?: T = P` an optional FIXED one, `w: T = P` is a FIXED field that is not\n      optional, and `w: T ~ P` is refused.\n\n      A field the template\'s `discriminators` names may be FIXED to a parameter,\n      optional or not as its name is marked (`type?: text = T`): each\n      application supplies its pin, and the base reads the field required with\n      that pin dropped. Its type is never a parameter, so the base reads it\n      without an application.\n      """\n    record_template_field => {\n      ( type: meta.field_type_ref | type_param: meta.param_name )\n      optional?: boolean ~ false\n      voidable?: boolean ~ false\n      role?:     meta.field_role ~ FREE\n      ( value: type! | value_param: meta.param_name )?\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The fields of a record template, as `meta.field_map` is a record\'s: each\n      field\'s name mapped to its facts, in declaration order.\n      """\n    record_template_field_map => !meta.map { key_type: text  value_type: meta.record_template_field  ordered: true }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A record: named fields, fixed by the type, with the field groups over\n      them. A record is closed — no field carries a parameter — and it is what\n      readers and writers are built for; a record with parameters is a\n      `meta.record_template`.\n\n      `extension` states how the record may be realised, and is OPEN unless the\n      record says otherwise. Of the constructors, only `record` and\n      `record_template` carry it.\n\n      Its chain is `type`\'s. `fields` is flattened: a composition\'s fields are\n      its operands\' and its own, merged, so a consumer that reads values against\n      it never repeats the merge.\n\n      `discriminators` names the fields this record\'s members are selected by,\n      in the order their pins are compared as a tuple, and its presence is what\n      makes the family member-dispatched rather than tag-dispatched. Every name\n      must name a field the enclosing record declares, and the record must be\n      ABSTRACT. The author writes `=?` on the field, which is what lowers into\n      this list ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2).\n\n      `name_type` names the family this record\'s field names belong to, as an\n      enum_type\'s names its members\': every name in `fields`, `discriminators`\n      and the groups is a value of it, and so is every field name a data value\n      of this record writes. It names an identifier family, a member of\n      `meta.identifier_ref`, so every field name is a name under its profile,\n      and the field name slots are therefore `text`, each judged by this type. A\n      record\'s name type is set where it is declared and a subtype cannot change\n      it: absent, it is its supertypes\' one name type, and `meta.identifier`,\n      which this document names `meta.field_name`, for a record with none; stated\n      beside supertypes, it is theirs. Supertypes with different name types do\n      not compose.\n      """\n    record => meta.product & {\n      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED\n      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = FIXED\n      fields:          meta.field_map\n      groups?:         [meta.field_group]\n      extension?:      meta.record_extension_type ~ OPEN\n      discriminators?: [text; 1..]\n      name_type?:      meta.identifier_ref\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A record template ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10): a record body over `parameters`,\n      whose fields are `meta.record_template_field`s and may carry them. Each\n      application substitutes its arguments for the parameters and closes to a\n      `meta.record`, its member.\n\n      A record template is a **family base**: a type a position may name,\n      ABSTRACT by construction, a value there being a value of one of its\n      members, each IS-A it. It takes part in IS-A as any abstract parent does,\n      and nothing reads or writes a value against it, so no reader or writer is\n      built for it: a reader at a position it types dispatches to a member and\n      reads against the member\'s closed record. That is why it is its own\n      constructor and not a record with an optional list of parameters.\n\n      A parameter stands only in a field\'s `type_param` or `value_param`, or as\n      an argument of an application in a field\'s `type` or in a parent; the\n      rest of the template is what every application shares. Its type and bound are derived\n      from where it stands, as `meta.template_param` says.\n\n      `extension` states how each application is realised, the template\'s own\n      being ABSTRACT whatever it says, so `result => abstract <T> { ... }` makes\n      `result<text>` abstract over its own members. `discriminators` is the\n      family\'s, as on an ABSTRACT record, and every field it names is typed by\n      no parameter, so the base reads each one without an application. `groups`\n      and `name_type` are each application\'s, unchanged by substitution.\n\n      Its chain is `type`\'s. A parent may still be open —\n      `<T> result<T> & { ... }` — and it is substituted and closed with every\n      other reference the template holds, so `ok<text>` reaches an IS-A edge to\n      `result<text>` and not to `result<int32>`. `fields` is flattened, as a\n      record\'s is.\n      """\n    record_template => meta.product & {\n      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED\n      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = FIXED\n      fields:          meta.record_template_field_map\n      groups?:         [meta.field_group]\n      extension?:      meta.record_extension_type ~ OPEN\n      discriminators?: [text; 1..]\n      name_type?:      meta.identifier_ref\n      parameters:      [meta.template_param; 1..]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A sequence of elements of one type. The container constructors carry no\n      parameters: a type slot (array\'s element_type, map\'s key_type and\n      value_type) is an ordinary required field typed `meta.type_ref`, filled\n      by the construction or by a sugar form\'s desugaring like any other\n      required field, and a closed application carries the bound reference as\n      the field\'s value (`!meta.array { element_type: person }`), its binding\n      record being ordinary data of this vocabulary. Inside a held template body\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10) any slot — type, scalar, or collection — may hold a\n      parameter token, substituted away before the body is read against this\n      vocabulary. `voidable` says whether an element may be void, spelled\n      `[T?]`.\n\n      `ordered` says whether order is part of a container\'s value: true for an\n      array unless it says otherwise, false for a set. It never changes what a\n      document may write, and output keeps the order written either way\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.5) — the facet says whether two values differing only in\n      order are one value.\n      """\n    array => meta.product & {\n      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = INDEX\n      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = VARIABLE\n      element_type:    meta.type_ref\n      voidable?:       boolean ~ false\n      ordered?:        boolean ~ true\n      unique_items?:   boolean ~ false\n      min_items?:      numeric.count\n      max_items?:      numeric.count\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The unordered, unique refinement of array. Its bounds are array\'s: the\n      empty set is a set, and a set that must be non-empty says `min_items: 1`.\n\n      The template an author writes is the root\'s `set`,\n      `set => <T> !meta.set_type { element_type: T }`, so a field reads\n      `tags: set<text>`.\n      """\n    set_type => meta.array ^ {\n      voidable?:     = false\n      ordered?:      = false\n      unique_items?: = true\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Entries keyed by distinct values of one type. `voidable` says whether an\n      entry\'s value may be void, spelled `{K => V?}`, as array\'s says it of an\n      element; a key is never void.\n\n      `ordered` is false unless the map says otherwise, as for a JSON object:\n      two maps holding the same entries in different orders are one value. An\n      ordered map, `!meta.map { … ordered: true }`, is one whose entry order is\n      part of its value, so those two are distinct, and a host binds it to a map\n      that keeps its order. Either way output keeps the order written\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.5).\n      """\n    map => meta.product & {\n      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED\n      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = VARIABLE\n      key_type:        meta.type_ref\n      value_type:      meta.type_ref\n      voidable?:       boolean ~ false\n      ordered?:        boolean ~ false\n      min_items?:      numeric.count\n      max_items?:      numeric.count\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A map keyed by names: the constructor of a namespace type, a refinement\n      of `map` whose `key_type` narrows from `meta.type_ref` to\n      `meta.identifier_ref`, which is IS-A it, and whose entries are never\n      void. A namespace type is an instance, as a map type is one of `map`:\n      `api => !meta.namespace { key_type: meta.identifier  value_type: method }`,\n      and an entry holding a value of it, `orders => !api { ... }`, is an\n      ordinary value of the type `api`. Its keys are names, of an identifier\n      family, which is what a qualified name\'s segment is, and a segment selects\n      a key of a namespace value and of no other map.\n\n      `key_type` is the family every key is read and judged under. `ordered`\n      and the size bounds are map\'s. The schema map (`schema`) is the kernel\'s\n      one.\n      """\n    namespace => meta.map ^ {\n      key_type:  meta.identifier_ref\n      voidable?: = false\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      One position of a tuple: its type, and whether its value may be void. A\n      position is never missing, so `[A, B?]` admits `[a _]` and refuses `[a]`.\n      """\n    tuple_element => {\n      element_type: meta.type_ref\n      voidable?:    boolean ~ false\n    }\n\n    @doc:"A fixed sequence of positions, each with a type of its own."\n    tuple => meta.product & {\n      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = INDEX\n      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = FIXED\n      elements:        [meta.tuple_element]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A tuple of exactly one element: `meta.tuple1<text>` admits `[a]` and\n      refuses `[]`, `[a b]` and `[_]`. `[T]` is an array, and the tuple sugar\n      takes two or more positions.\n      """\n    tuple1 => <T> !meta.tuple { elements: [{ element_type: T }] }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A tuple of exactly one voidable element: as `meta.tuple1`, and the element\n      may be void, so `meta.voidable_tuple1<text>` admits `[a]` and `[_]`.\n      """\n    voidable_tuple1 => <T> !meta.tuple { elements: [{ element_type: T  voidable: true }] }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The members of an enum_type. The set\'s own uniqueness compares the text as\n      written; each member is then read as a value of the enum\'s `name_type`,\n      and two that are one value under that type\'s equality are a duplicate.\n      """\n    enum_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: text  min_items: 1 }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      An enumeration: a closed set of names, and the type they are drawn from\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.4), as a record\'s `name_type` is its field names\'.\n      `name_type` names an identifier family — a member of\n      `meta.identifier_ref`, a type built with identifier_type — and each\n      member must be a value of it, distinct from the others under its equality,\n      and is a name under its profile ([TSON-DATA] §8.2). A closed set of text\n      that is no name is the text family\'s own `members` facet,\n      `!text ^ { members: [...] }`; an enumeration of names a profile admits\n      beyond the kernel\'s identifier, such as words separated by single spaces,\n      is an enum_type over the author\'s own identifier_type.\n\n      A value `name_type` takes from a constructor resolves where that\n      constructor is declared; one an author writes resolves in the author\'s own\n      namespace, so a schema may enumerate names of a naming vocabulary it\n      declares itself. `name_type` is fixed once an enum is constructed: a\n      refinement narrows the members and never the type.\n      """\n    enum_type => meta.atom & {\n      name_type: meta.identifier_ref\n      members:   meta.enum_set\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Closed sum. `disjoint` is a resolver-derived index over the variant list,\n      parallel to `subtypes`: true or false by discrimination-class distinctness\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4). Declarations never set it; on ingest it MUST be\n      discarded and recomputed. It carries the encoding-independent fact each\n      encoding\'s discrimination rules consume ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4, §7.2).\n      """\n    choice => meta.sum & {\n      variants:  [meta.type_ref]\n      disjoint?: boolean\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Author\'s assertion that a choice\'s variants are distinguishable by an\n      encoding\'s form resolution — discrimination-class distinctness, not\n      disjointness of inhabitance — checked against the resolver\'s derived\n      `disjoint` fact in the `!meta.choice` body ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4, §8.1). The\n      fact is class-stable: a variant whose forms straddle classes (an\n      approximate atom still admitting NaN or the infinities, a map with a\n      compound key) has no class, so the untagged values of a choice are one set\n      in every encoding. A void-targeted presence marker like `@atom.numeric`:\n      written bare, and presence is the assertion. Carries no decode force — the\n      resolver computes disjointness whether or not the annotation is present.\n      The derivation is total: present and refuted is a schema-load error;\n      present and verified, or absent, is silent.\n      """\n    disjoint => @meta.annotation void\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Constructor for the open sum over the governing closure: the value names\n      its own type by its tag, among the types of the closure ([TSON-SCHEMA]\n      §2.2.3), and is validated in full against it. A value naming no type is\n      invalid. Where `choice` is the closed sum, enumerating its variants, this\n      is the open one, drawing them from the closure; `disjoint` is absent, as\n      on every non-choice sum.\n      """\n    declared_type => meta.sum & {}\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A value of a type the closure declares, chosen by the value: the tag names\n      the type, the name resolves in the closure exactly as it does anywhere\n      else, and the value is validated in full against it. What a schema map\'s\n      entries are.\n      """\n    declared => !meta.declared_type {}\n\n    @doc:"""\n      A name held in a slot: an atom whose values are names, each naming an\n      entry whose value\'s type, past the entry\'s alias hops ([TSON-SCHEMA]\n      §8.3), is `of` or IS-A it. Every entry is a name and a value, and the\n      value has a type, its tag: `order => { ... }` holds a value of `record`,\n      `int32 => !integer ^ { ... }` a value of `integer_type`, and\n      `orders => !api { ... }` a value of `api`. A field\'s type position, a\n      `meta.ref<meta.record>` slot and a `meta.ref<method>` slot all admit a\n      name by that one predicate, and differ only in `of`.\n\n      `of` names any type or constructor of the writer\'s namespace\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §3.3.1), never a value or an open entry. Where it is a\n      constructor, `top` or IS-A it, the values it admits are types, and a slot\n      holds a type\'s name, which takes part in structural identity\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2). A record written in braces is built with `record`,\n      and so is `top` itself and a constructor whose declaration is a record, so\n      `meta.ref<meta.record>` holds all of them; a record template is built with\n      `record_template` and is not among them. A template over another\n      constructor is no type, and an application of one is the type it expands\n      to. Where `of` is any other type, the values it admits\n      are values, and a slot holds a value\'s qualified name: the first segment\n      resolves in the writer\'s namespace and names an entry, and each further\n      segment selects a key of a namespace value or a field of a record value.\n      A map that is no namespace is not entered.\n\n      A type\'s name is its entry\'s path from the document\'s root: `order`, or\n      `geo.address` for a member of a schema map nested as\n      `geo => !schema { ... }`, and every name resolves from the root.\n\n      A value is the name, never a value of the type it names. In a data\n      document it resolves in the namespace of the governing schema and is\n      judged there. In a schema document its grammar is read where it is\n      written, and the name is judged when that schema links: the set of names\n      a reference admits is the closure\'s, derived and never written.\n\n      A value may carry an argument list, `geo.address<T>`: after its qualified\n      name, an adjacent `<`, one or more arguments, and `>`. It is data grammar\n      ([TSON-DATA] §7.4), written unquoted wherever a value stands. An argument\n      is a reference or a token, positional against the head\'s parameters, and\n      typed where it lands: a type argument is judged as the parameter\'s `bound`\n      says, and a value argument is a token of the parameter\'s type\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10). The canonical text has no whitespace but one space\n      between arguments: `pair<text integer>`, `maxint<10>`. Identity is the\n      parsed form ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2), so whitespace inside an argument list is\n      free. Every application denotes an entry: a closed one lifts to the entry\n      it denotes and is named bare where it stands ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.3), so a\n      value carries arguments only where an application is still open, in a\n      record template\'s `parents` and field types, in held text, and in the\n      `application` annotation.\n\n      A refinement narrows `of`: the refined `of` is the source\'s or IS-A it,\n      so every name the refinement admits the source admits too, and the\n      refinement is IS-A its source. `meta.identifier_ref` is `meta.type_ref`\n      narrowed to the identifier families.\n\n      A value may instead be a sibling reference, `kind!` ([TSON-DATA] §7.4):\n      one identifier with an adjacent `!` followed by whitespace, naming the\n      field `kind` of the same record instance, whose value names the type\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2). A reference is a name, an application or a sibling\n      reference, and `allow_sibling` says whether the last is admitted: as\n      `allow_nan` is of `numeric.float_type`, it is a permission, true by\n      default, and a refinement may withdraw it and never grant it back. It is\n      true only at a record field\'s type, `meta.field_type_ref`; every other\n      reference sets it false and refuses a sibling reference. Resolved output\n      writes a sibling reference as it is written.\n      """\n    reference_type => meta.atom & {\n      of:             meta.type_ref\n      allow_sibling?: boolean ~ true\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The spelling of a reference: `meta.ref<T>` is\n      `!meta.reference_type { of: T  allow_sibling: false }`. Where `T` is a\n      constructor it holds the names of the types built with it,\n      `meta.ref<net.uuid_type>`, and otherwise the names of values of `T`,\n      `meta.ref<method>`. It never holds a sibling reference.\n      """\n    ref => <T> !meta.reference_type { of: T  allow_sibling: false }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The type of a record field: the name of a type a value can have, or a\n      sibling reference, `kind!`, naming the field of the same record whose\n      value names the field\'s type ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2). `record_field.type`\n      and `record_template_field.type` are typed by it, and no other slot is.\n      """\n    field_type_ref => !meta.reference_type { of: meta.type }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The names of the types a value can have: `meta.field_type_ref` without\n      the sibling reference, a refinement of it, so a type reference stands\n      wherever a field\'s type can.\n      """\n    type_ref => !meta.field_type_ref ^ { allow_sibling: false }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The names of the identifier families, which a `name_type` names: a\n      refinement of `meta.type_ref`, an identifier family being a type.\n      """\n    identifier_ref => !meta.type_ref ^ { of: unicode.identifier_type }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Resolver output: a schema map, from each name to the value its entry\n      resolves to, a type or a value, each naming its type by its tag. Resolved\n      output and source are one grammar — output is source with sugar expanded,\n      bodies flattened and chains computed, and an entry written as a\n      constructor application resolves to itself. A value of `schema` nested as\n      an entry, `geo => !schema { ... }`, declares as the root does: its members\n      are entries of the document, each named by its path from the root,\n      `geo.address`. A map of one path declared by several documents of a\n      closure is one map, the union of their members, and a member\'s identity\n      is its declaring document and its path; a member path two of them\n      declare collides as any name does ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3). The\n      annotations written on a map\'s entry are its declaring document\'s.\n      """\n    schema => !meta.namespace { key_type: meta.type_name  value_type: meta.declared }\n  }\n\n  binary => !schema {\n    @doc:"""\n      The base encodings of RFC 4648 that a text encoding may spell a `bytes`\n      value in (HEX is §8\'s base16).\n      """\n    bytes_encoding => !enum [BASE64 BASE64URL BASE32 HEX]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Octet-sequence constraint vocabulary. Instance is `bytes`, at the root. The\n      value is the octets: equality, identity, content addressing and the length\n      facets are all over octets, never over a spelling — `length: 32` is a\n      32-byte digest whether it arrives as 64 hex characters, 44 base64\n      characters, or 32 raw bytes — so a round trip through any encoding\n      preserves the value.\n\n      `encoding` is a selector ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.7): it picks which RFC 4648\n      alphabet the text class of encodings — TSON text, JSON, and any encoding\n      whose values are character sequences — spells the octets in, and an\n      encoding whose values are octets ignores it. A selector picks a spelling,\n      so it never changes what two values compare as. It defaults to BASE64 (§4,\n      padded), so a `bytes` position is base64 in every text encoding.\n\n      `encoding` is not refinable. Another alphabet is another type, declared as\n      its own instance:\n      `hexdigest => !binary.bytes_type { encoding: HEX  length: 4 }`. Refining\n      for length inherits the alphabet: `sha256 => !bytes ^ { length: 32 }` is a\n      base64 sha256.\n      """\n    bytes_type => meta.atom & {\n      encoding?:   binary.bytes_encoding ~ BASE64\n      length?:     numeric.count\n      min_length?: numeric.count\n      max_length?: numeric.count\n    }\n  }\n\n  void => !meta.void_type {}\n\n  @doc:"The two-valued type of a constraint field that is a switch."\n  boolean => !enum [true false]\n\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:false @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  integer => !numeric.integer_type {}\n\n  @doc:"Signed 64-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  int64 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 64  signed: true } }\n\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  text => !unicode.text_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Octet sequence. The universal binary type, and the only one: a field is\n    `avatar: bytes`, a digest `sha256 => !bytes ^ { length: 32 }`. Equality and\n    the length facets are over octets; the type says nothing about spelling. The\n    text class of encodings spells `bytes` in base64 (RFC 4648 §4). Another\n    alphabet is another type, declared as its own instance of the constructor\n    (`hexdigest => !binary.bytes_type { encoding: HEX  length: 4 }`) — never as\n    a refinement of this one. An encoding whose values are octets writes them\n    raw.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  bytes => !binary.bytes_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    An IRI-reference (RFC 3987 §2.2): an IRI or a relative reference, its\n    characters drawn from RFC 3987\'s grammar, beyond US-ASCII included.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  iri_reference => !net.iri_type {}\n\n  @doc:"An IRI (RFC 3987 §2.2): the scheme is required."\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  iri => !iri_reference ^ { allow_relative: false }\n\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  regex => !unicode.regex_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Documentation for a declaration, a field or a document. The text is\n    CommonMark 0.31.2 (https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/) with no extensions.\n    Every string is valid CommonMark, so this refuses nothing and says only how\n    the text is displayed. Raw HTML in it is never executed, and a renderer need\n    not render it.\n    """\n  doc => @meta.annotation text\n\n  @doc:"""\n    An enumeration of identifiers, the enumeration constructor an author\n    writes bare: the positional `!enum [OPEN DONE]`.\n    """\n  enum => meta.enum_type ^ { name_type?: = meta.identifier }\n\n  @doc:"""\n    The set spelling an author writes: `set<text>` where the bare\n    construction is `!meta.set_type { element_type: text }`. A §5.10\n    template, not a constructor: it composes with nothing and so is not IS-A\n    `top`.\n    """\n  set => <T> !meta.set_type { element_type: T }\n\n  @doc:"""\n    The tag of a schema map, an alias of `meta.schema`: a schema document\'s\n    root, `!schema { … }`, and a map nested as an entry,\n    `geo => !schema { … }`.\n    """\n  schema => meta.schema\n}\n';

/** `spec/m/numeric.tn`'s own header: `!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/numeric.tn?sha256=dfa66c5d3775a7845776db737f07b16630824f2434f6587aface2f88b77bf0f2"` */
export const NUMERIC_TN =
  '!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/numeric.tn?sha256=dfa66c5d3775a7845776db737f07b16630824f2434f6587aface2f88b77bf0f2"\n!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=c9b069b606f7cc8a043ebabe3fe91e4aaa35a9ffd814b68be0ba4bbe6d02bfb5"\n@doc:"""\n  TSON numeric — the numeric type library (2026 Revision 39 draft).\n\n  Its root holds the numbers a data schema writes: the exact `number` and\n  `rational`, `complex`, the sized integers `int8` to `int256` (but `int64`,\n  the kernel\'s) and `uint8` to `uint256`, and the approximate `float32` and\n  `float64`. Their constructors\n  and facet vocabulary are machinery, members of `numeric`, a schema map\n  nested in its root, each named by its path: `numeric.decimal_type`,\n  `numeric.float_type`, `numeric.rational_type` and `numeric.complex_type`,\n  the facet enums `numeric.ieee_format` and `numeric.complex_component`, and\n  the member set `numeric.decimal_member_set`. The kernel contributes to the\n  same map: `numeric.integer_type`, the constructor of the kernel\'s\n  `integer`, which the sized integers refine, its facet vocabulary, and\n  `numeric.count`.\n\n  It imports the kernel, meta.tn, alone: the kernel\'s constructors,\n  `numeric.count` and classifying annotations are what it builds on, and it\n  needs nothing of temporal.tn or net.tn. A schema\n  importing it reserves its root names beside the kernel\'s, `numeric` among\n  those ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3). core.tn imports it with temporal.tn and\n  net.tn, for a schema that wants every bundled type.\n\n  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see meta.tn\'s\n  note.\n\n  An annotation never changes a value, its type, or whether it is valid:\n  validation is over values, and a schema with every annotation erased admits\n  exactly the same values. [TSON-SCHEMA] §6 admits two further things within\n  that rule — a load-time check, and a directive over how a class of encodings\n  represents a value — and the annotations used here use the first alone.\n  A value space is described without reference to any encoding, and where a\n  doc mentions how TSON text spells a value it says so by name.\n\n  Constructors that bind to a governing specification compose with the\n  kernel\'s `atom.specification` mixin and pin the spec field with `=`.\n\n  A constraint field whose value is of the constrained family — a bound, a\n  step, a member — is typed by that family\'s instance at this document\'s\n  root: `number` for the exact tier and for a float\'s bounds, `rational` for\n  the rational tier. A field that counts something — a digit count — is typed\n  by the kernel\'s `numeric.count`.\n\n  The ordered families state their bounds as field groups ([TSON-SCHEMA]\n  §5.11): one inclusive-or-exclusive group per side, so an inclusive and an\n  exclusive bound on the same side is unrepresentable. Value-level coherence\n  (the lower bound not exceeding the upper) is a schema-load check.\n\n  `multiple_of`, wherever it appears (`numeric.decimal_type`,\n  `numeric.rational_type`, the kernel\'s `numeric.integer_type`, and\n  temporal.tn\'s `temporal.duration_type` and `temporal.period_type`), follows\n  one rule: the step is strictly positive — zero or a negative step is a\n  schema-load error — the test ignores the sign of the value, a refinement may\n  only tighten the step to an integer multiple of the inherited one, and the\n  step composes with every other facet present. `members`, where it appears, is likewise\n  uniform in what it admits: every member satisfies the other facets on the\n  same body or the schema fails to load. Refinement differs by tier — the\n  numeric tiers shrink the set, the kernel\'s `unicode.text_type` sets it once.\n\n  References:\n    ISO/IEC 11404:2007    General Purpose Datatypes\n    W3C XSD Part 2        Facet model and constraint vocabulary\n    JSON Schema 2020-12   Modern constraint vocabulary\n    IEEE 754-2019         Floating-point semantics\n  """\n!schema {\n  @doc:"The numeric constructors and their facet vocabulary, each named by its path, `numeric.decimal_type`."\n  numeric => !schema {\n    @doc:"""\n      IEEE 754-2019 interchange formats, both radices. Selects the representable\n      grid of an approximate (`float_type`) value. The binary formats are the\n      familiar base-2 floats; the decimal formats (decimal32/64/128) are base-10\n      *floating-point* — still approximate, with their own special values — and\n      are NOT the exact fixed-point decimals of `decimal_type`.\n      """\n    ieee_format => !enum [\n      BINARY16 BINARY32 BINARY64 BINARY128 BINARY256\n      DECIMAL32 DECIMAL64 DECIMAL128\n    ]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Approximate-numeric constraint vocabulary (SQL\'s approximate tier; ISO/IEC\n      11404 approximate real). Instances are `float32` and `float64`. An\n      approximate value is not stored as written: the receiver rounds it onto\n      the discrete IEEE 754-2019 grid named by `format`, so precision may be\n      lost and equality comparison is unsafe (ordering is PARTIAL — NaN is\n      unordered). The `format` radix (binary or decimal) is the ISO 11404 radix\n      characteristic of the type. The value set is that grid together with\n      signed zeros, subnormals, the two infinities, and the canonical quiet NaN\n      ([TSON-DATA] §5.6); `allow_nan`, `allow_infinity`, `allow_subnormal`, and\n      `allow_negative_zero` default to true (`~ true` — the format\'s full set)\n      and may be narrowed to false to exclude those representation artifacts.\n      The bounds are optional domain restrictions validated against the value as\n      written, before rounding, so they are exact `number`s rather than values\n      on the grid; they judge the infinities as ordered values and admit no\n      NaN, so `allow_nan` decides NaN only where no bound is written\n      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5, §5.7). Not a narrowing of the exact tier:\n      infinities and NaN are not exact numbers.\n\n      Bounds follow the header\'s field-group rule. There is no `multiple_of`;\n      use the exact tier (`decimal_type`) when a step is required. A text-class\n      token that does not land on the grid is rounded to nearest, ties to even\n      (IEEE 754-2019 roundTiesToEven), the default across the format; loss of\n      precision is expected, not an error. An encoding that carries the format\'s\n      bits delivers a grid value directly and rounds nothing.\n      """\n    float_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:                = "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8766229"\n      format:               numeric.ieee_format\n      ( min: number | exclusive_min: number )?\n      ( max: number | exclusive_max: number )?\n      allow_nan?:           boolean ~ true\n      allow_infinity?:      boolean ~ true\n      allow_subnormal?:     boolean ~ true\n      allow_negative_zero?: boolean ~ true\n    }\n\n    @doc:"The members of a decimal_type."\n    decimal_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: number  min_items: 1 }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Exact-numeric constraint vocabulary (SQL\'s exact tier; ISO/IEC 11404\n      `scaled`, radix 10). Instance is `number` — the unrestricted exact\n      number, which is also the JSON Schema `number` mapping (arbitrary\n      magnitude and precision, finite, no special values). Nothing is rounded on\n      the way in, so equality is safe and ordering is TOTAL. Scale is not part\n      of the value — `1`, `1.0` and `1.00` are one value, and whether a\n      spelling\'s trailing zeros survive a round trip is an encoding\'s promise,\n      not the type\'s. `total_digits`/`fraction_digits` are the ISO `scaled`\n      precision and scale (SQL `DECIMAL(precision, scale)`): total significant\n      digits and digits after the point.\n\n      Bounds follow the header\'s field-group rule. `multiple_of` is exact here\n      (e.g. `multiple_of` 0.05 admits only nickel steps). `fraction_digits` and\n      `multiple_of` overlap but are not equivalent: `fraction_digits` 2 admits\n      any hundredth, `multiple_of` 0.05 only multiples of a nickel.\n\n      `members` enumerates the admitted values. Each member is a `number`, so\n      `1` and `1.0` are one member and a duplicate rather than two, and a\n      member that is not a number fails at schema load. The header\'s uniform\n      `members` rule applies.\n      """\n    decimal_type => meta.atom & {\n      ( min: number | exclusive_min: number )?\n      ( max: number | exclusive_max: number )?\n      multiple_of?:     number\n      total_digits?:    numeric.count\n      fraction_digits?: numeric.count\n      members?:         numeric.decimal_member_set\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Rational constraint vocabulary. Instance is `rational`. Exact (ℚ;\n      ISO/IEC 11404 `rational`), so bounds and `multiple_of` are exact. Bounds\n      follow the header\'s field-group rule. Constraints operate on the value,\n      not the written token: tokens are not normalized, so `"2/4"` and `"1/2"`\n      satisfy the same constraints.\n      """\n    rational_type => meta.atom & {\n      ( min: rational | exclusive_min: rational )?\n      ( max: rational | exclusive_max: rational )?\n      multiple_of?: rational\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Allowed component types for a complex value. A closed vocabulary of\n      numeric types (naming their counterparts at this document\'s root): exact `INTEGER` (Gaussian\n      integers ℤ+ℤi), `NUMBER` (exact arbitrary precision), `RATIONAL` (ℚ+ℚi);\n      approximate `FLOAT32`, `FLOAT64`.\n\n      The members are a partial order, not a chain, and a refinement may move\n      `component` only to a member whose value set is a subset of the source\'s:\n      `INTEGER ⊂ NUMBER ⊂ RATIONAL` are the exact tiers and `FLOAT32 ⊂ FLOAT64`\n      the approximate ones, and the two families are incomparable. So\n      `!complex ^ { component: INTEGER }` narrows and its IS-A holds, while\n      `!complex ^ { component: FLOAT64 }` is refused: a float64 complex is not a\n      `complex`, and is declared as its own type,\n      `!numeric.complex_type { component: FLOAT64 }`.\n      """\n    complex_component => !enum [INTEGER NUMBER RATIONAL FLOAT32 FLOAT64]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Complex constraint vocabulary. Instance is `complex`. A complex\n      value is a pair of components (real and imaginary parts). `component`\n      selects the type of both parts from the closed `numeric.complex_component`\n      vocabulary; it defaults to `NUMBER` (a DEFAULT, §5.2), so a bare complex\n      has exact components — a defaulted selector. Complex has no useful total\n      order (`@atom.ordering:NONE`), so the order-based facets of the other\n      numeric tiers do not apply here. Exactness is inherited from `component`\n      rather than fixed: `INTEGER`/`NUMBER`/`RATIONAL` components give an exact\n      complex, `FLOAT32`/`FLOAT64` an approximate one, so `complex` carries no\n      `@atom.exact` marker of its own.\n      """\n    complex_type => meta.atom & {\n      component?: numeric.complex_component ~ NUMBER\n    }\n  }\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Exact number of arbitrary magnitude and precision, finite, with no special\n    values (SQL exact tier; ISO/IEC 11404 `scaled`, radix 10). The canonical\n    numeric type and the JSON Schema `number` mapping: a bare JSON number is a\n    TSON `number`. Exact, so equality is safe and ordering is TOTAL; scale is\n    not part of the value (`1`, `1.0` and `1.00` are one value). Lossiness\n    enters only when a consumer narrows to an approximate type\n    (`float32`/`float64`), where the value is rounded onto an IEEE grid. A value\n    needing `.inf`/`.nan` is not a `number` — those belong to the approximate\n    tier.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:false @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  number => !numeric.decimal_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Exact fraction a/b with a positive nonzero denominator. The value is the\n    fraction: 2/4 and 1/2 are one value, and equality, ordering and constraints\n    are over it; receivers reduce if their representation needs it. Whether a\n    spelling survives a round trip is an encoding\'s promise, not the type\'s\n    (TSON text preserves the token as written, and quotes it, since it contains\n    `/`).\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:false @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  rational => !numeric.rational_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Complex number, a pair of components (real and imaginary parts). Components\n    default to `NUMBER` (exact). A refinement may move `component` only to a\n    member whose value set is a subset of the source\'s:\n    `!complex ^ { component: INTEGER }` is Gaussian integers, ℤ+ℤi sitting\n    inside the exact-decimal complexes, while floating-point complex,\n    `!numeric.complex_type { component: FLOAT64 }`, is its own type rather than a\n    refinement and is not a `complex`. Exactness follows the component type, so\n    `complex` carries no `@atom.exact` marker. Spellings are the text class\'s\n    concern (TSON text accepts a+bi and a+bj); component representation is\n    host-defined.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false @atom.numeric\n  complex => !numeric.complex_type {}\n\n  @doc:"Signed 8-bit integer (-128 to 127)."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  int8 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 8  signed: true } }\n\n  @doc:"Signed 16-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  int16 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 16  signed: true } }\n\n  @doc:"Signed 32-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  int32 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 32  signed: true } }\n\n  @doc:"Signed 128-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  int128 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 128  signed: true } }\n\n  @doc:"Signed 256-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  int256 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 256  signed: true } }\n\n  @doc:"Unsigned 8-bit integer (0 to 255)."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  uint8 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 8  signed: false } }\n\n  @doc:"Unsigned 16-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  uint16 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 16  signed: false } }\n\n  @doc:"Unsigned 32-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  uint32 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 32  signed: false } }\n\n  @doc:"Unsigned 64-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  uint64 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 64  signed: false } }\n\n  @doc:"Unsigned 128-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  uint128 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 128  signed: false } }\n\n  @doc:"Unsigned 256-bit integer."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric\n  uint256 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 256  signed: false } }\n\n  @doc:"""\n    IEEE 754 binary32. Value set is the binary32 grid plus signed zeros,\n    subnormals, both infinities, and quiet NaN.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:PARTIAL @atom.bounded:false @atom.exact:false @atom.numeric\n  float32 => !numeric.float_type { format: BINARY32 }\n\n  @doc:"""\n    IEEE 754 binary64. Value set is the binary64 grid plus signed zeros,\n    subnormals, both infinities, and quiet NaN.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:PARTIAL @atom.bounded:false @atom.exact:false @atom.numeric\n  float64 => !numeric.float_type { format: BINARY64 }\n}\n';

/** `spec/m/temporal.tn`'s own header: `!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/temporal.tn?sha256=fb4ada8f238de82821e7fcf512c21980a8b73c8df9260cdf295ba0157cc3971e"` */
export const TEMPORAL_TN =
  '!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/temporal.tn?sha256=fb4ada8f238de82821e7fcf512c21980a8b73c8df9260cdf295ba0157cc3971e"\n!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=c9b069b606f7cc8a043ebabe3fe91e4aaa35a9ffd814b68be0ba4bbe6d02bfb5"\n@doc:"""\n  TSON temporal — the temporal type library (2026 Revision 39 draft).\n\n  Its root holds the temporal types a data schema writes: `date`, `time`,\n  `datetime`, `duration` and `period`. Their constructors are machinery,\n  members of `temporal`, a schema map nested in its root, each named by its\n  path: `temporal.date_type`, `temporal.time_type`, `temporal.datetime_type`,\n  `temporal.duration_type` and `temporal.period_type`.\n\n  It imports the kernel, meta.tn, alone: the kernel\'s constructors,\n  `numeric.count` and classifying annotations are what it builds on, and it\n  needs nothing of numeric.tn or net.tn. A schema\n  importing it reserves `temporal` and its root names beside the kernel\'s\n  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3), and sees none of numeric.tn\'s: importing temporal.tn\n  gives `date` and no `int32`. core.tn imports it with numeric.tn and net.tn,\n  for a schema that wants every bundled type.\n\n  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see meta.tn\'s\n  note.\n\n  An annotation never changes a value, its type, or whether it is valid:\n  validation is over values, and a schema with every annotation erased admits\n  exactly the same values. A value space is described without reference to\n  any encoding, and where a doc mentions how TSON text spells a value it says\n  so by name.\n\n  Every constructor here binds to a governing specification, RFC 3339: it\n  composes with the kernel\'s `atom.specification` mixin and pins the\n  spec field with `=`.\n\n  A bound or a step is of the constrained family and is typed by that\n  family\'s instance at this document\'s root: a `date_type` bound is a `date`,\n  a `period_type` step a `period`. A precision counts digits and is typed by\n  the kernel\'s `numeric.count`. The bounds are field groups\n  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.11): one inclusive-or-exclusive group per side, so an\n  inclusive and an exclusive bound on the same side is unrepresentable.\n  Value-level coherence (the lower bound not exceeding the upper) is a\n  schema-load check.\n\n  `multiple_of`, on `temporal.duration_type` and `temporal.period_type`,\n  follows the one rule numeric.tn states for every step: strictly positive,\n  sign-blind, tightened by a refinement only to an integer multiple of the\n  inherited step, and composed with every other facet present.\n\n  References:\n    RFC 3339              Internet date/time profile; Appendix A is\n                          the duration/period grammar (ISO 8601-1:2019\n                          is the informative source behind it)\n    ISO/IEC 11404:2007    General Purpose Datatypes\n    W3C XSD Part 2        Facet model and constraint vocabulary\n  """\n!schema {\n  @doc:"The temporal constructors, each named by its path, `temporal.date_type`."\n  temporal => !schema {\n    @doc:"Date constraint vocabulary. Instance is `date`."\n    date_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?: = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"\n      ( min: date | exclusive_min: date )?\n      ( max: date | exclusive_max: date )?\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Time constraint vocabulary. Instance is `time`. `precision` is a\n      constraint on the value, not on a spelling: `precision: N` admits a value\n      that is a whole number of 10^-N seconds, so `precision: 3` is millisecond\n      resolution and `precision: 0` whole seconds. The atom is exact — nothing\n      is truncated; a value off the grid is rejected ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).\n      Reading admits any spelling of an admitted value, trailing zeros included,\n      so `12:00:00.500` is admitted under `precision: 1`; a text encoding\n      writing the value writes at most N fractional digits, `12:00:00.5`.\n      """\n    time_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"\n      ( min: time | exclusive_min: time )?\n      ( max: time | exclusive_max: time )?\n      precision?: numeric.count\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Datetime constraint vocabulary. Instance is `datetime`.\n      `precision` as for `time_type`: the value is a whole number of 10^-N\n      seconds, a constraint on the value ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).\n      """\n    datetime_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"\n      ( min: datetime | exclusive_min: datetime )?\n      ( max: datetime | exclusive_max: datetime )?\n      precision?: numeric.count\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Elapsed-time constraint vocabulary. Instance is `duration`.\n      Lexical form is the RFC 3339 Appendix A `duration` production with three\n      extensions and one restriction: an optional leading `-`; a fraction on the\n      seconds component only, written with `.` (`PT0.5S`, never `PT1.5H` or\n      `PT0,5S`); any subset of D, H, M, S may be omitted provided at least one\n      component is present and order is kept (`PT1H30S` is admitted); and the Y\n      and month-M components are not admitted — `P1M` is one month and belongs\n      to `period`; a minute is `PT1M`. `PnW` stands alone, as in the ABNF\n      (`P1W2D` is an error). A day is exactly 86400 s and a week 7 days.\n\n      The value is a signed exact decimal number of seconds — numeric.tn\'s\n      `number`\'s value space, in seconds: nothing is rounded. So `PT90M`, `PT1H30M` and\n      `P0DT5400S` are one value, `PT0S`, `P0D` and `-PT0S` are one value, and\n      ordering is TOTAL. Bounds compare the value, not the token.\n\n      Both ends of that space are fixed at a signed 64-bit count of nanoseconds.\n      The seconds component admits at most nine fractional digits, and a\n      magnitude may not exceed 2^63 - 1 nanoseconds (about 292 years) — stated\n      as a magnitude, so negating an admitted duration always yields an admitted\n      one. A processor MUST represent every value in that range and MUST reject\n      one outside it whether or not its own representation could hold it.\n\n      `precision` as for `time_type`, and it is `fraction_digits` on the seconds\n      count: the value is a whole number of 10^-N seconds, so `precision: 0`\n      admits whole seconds only and `precision: 9` is the nanosecond grid. A\n      constraint on the value and not on a spelling — `PT0.50S` is a whole\n      number of tenths — and it may not exceed 9. `multiple_of` is\n      `numeric.decimal_type`\'s `multiple_of` on that same count, under the\n      header\'s uniform rule: `PT15M` admits only quarter-hour values.\n      """\n    duration_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339#appendix-A"\n      ( min: duration | exclusive_min: duration )?\n      ( max: duration | exclusive_max: duration )?\n      precision?:  numeric.count\n      multiple_of?: duration\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Calendar-span constraint vocabulary. Instance is `period`. Lexical\n      form is the RFC 3339 Appendix A `duration` production restricted to an\n      optional leading `-`, then `P` followed by a Y component, an M component,\n      or both in that order; no fraction, no W or D component and no `T` part\n      (`P1M15D`, `P1Y0D` and `P1YT1H` are errors — use `duration`, or a record\n      with a `period` field and a `duration` field, for mixed spans). The value\n      is a signed integer number of months, so `P1Y` and `P12M` are equal,\n      `P0Y`, `P0M` and `-P0M` are equal, and ordering is TOTAL. Bounds compare\n      the value, not the token. `multiple_of` is a period under the header\'s\n      uniform rule: `P3M` admits only whole quarters.\n      """\n    period_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339#appendix-A"\n      ( min: period | exclusive_min: period )?\n      ( max: period | exclusive_max: period )?\n      multiple_of?: period\n    }\n  }\n\n  @doc:"Calendar date. RFC 3339 full-date YYYY-MM-DD."\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true\n  date => !temporal.date_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Time of day. RFC 3339 full-time HH:MM:SS[.s…] with a mandatory offset. The\n    value is the time of day in UTC, on the finite interval [00:00:00,\n    24:00:00), so `10:00:00+01:00` and `09:00:00Z` are one value, and ordering\n    is TOTAL. Normalising to UTC wraps at midnight (`23:30:00-02:00` is\n    `01:30:00Z`): a local time of day — "09:00 wherever you are" — is not this\n    type. Equality and bounds compare the value. The offset is a spelling: TSON\n    text preserves it as written, an encoding that stores the instant may drop\n    it. A leap second, `23:59:60`, which RFC 3339\'s grammar admits, lies outside\n    the interval and is refused.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true\n  time => !temporal.time_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Date and time. RFC 3339 date-time with a mandatory offset. The value is the\n    instant on the UTC timeline, so `2026-01-01T10:00:00+01:00` and\n    `2026-01-01T09:00:00Z` are one value, and ordering is TOTAL. An offset of\n    `-00:00` (offset unknown, RFC 3339 §4.3) is the same instant as `Z` and\n    compares as such. A local datetime — a wall clock with no offset — is not\n    this type. Equality and bounds compare the value. The offset is a spelling:\n    TSON text preserves it as written, an encoding that stores the instant may\n    drop it, and a value for which the offset is data — an appointment shown in\n    its booked zone — is a different value space, not this type. A leap second,\n    second 60, which RFC 3339\'s grammar admits, is not an instant on that\n    timeline and is refused.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true\n  datetime => !temporal.datetime_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Elapsed time — a signed exact decimal number of seconds, within the range\n    `duration_type` fixes. No Y or month-M component, so every value has a fixed\n    length and ordering is TOTAL. `P1M` is a month and belongs to `period`; a\n    minute is `PT1M`. A day is exactly 86400 s and a week 7 days, so `PT90M`,\n    `PT1H30M` and `P0DT5400S` are one value.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true\n  duration => !temporal.duration_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Calendar span — a signed integer number of months. `P1Y` and `P12M` are one\n    value. A span that is genuinely both is a record with a field of each.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:false\n  period => !temporal.period_type {}\n}';

/** `spec/m/net.tn`'s own header: `!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/net.tn?sha256=371fc20c8628b5461b56a7a28f2559a1cfde4eda718f72d60ceb1b5a007bac06"` */
export const NET_TN =
  '!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/net.tn?sha256=371fc20c8628b5461b56a7a28f2559a1cfde4eda718f72d60ceb1b5a007bac06"\n!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=c9b069b606f7cc8a043ebabe3fe91e4aaa35a9ffd814b68be0ba4bbe6d02bfb5"\n@doc:"""\n  TSON net — the network and identifier type library (2026 Revision 39\n  draft).\n\n  Its root holds the network and identifier types a data schema writes:\n  `email`, `ipv4`, `ipv6`, `cidr4`, `cidr6`, `mac`, `hostname`, `host`,\n  `media_type`, `uri_reference`, `uri` and `uuid`. Their constructors are\n  machinery, members of `net`, a schema map nested in its root, each named by\n  its path: `net.ipv4_type`, `net.hostname_type`, `net.uri_type`,\n  `net.uuid_type`. The kernel contributes to the same map: `net.iri_type`,\n  the constructor of the kernel\'s `iri`, and its schemes, `net.scheme_set`\n  and `net.scheme_name`. The instances are empty, but for `uri`, which\n  refines `uri_reference` to require a scheme. The schemaless annotations of\n  the same names ([TSON-DATA] §5.5) denote these types.\n\n  It imports the kernel, meta.tn, alone: the kernel\'s constructors,\n  `atom.specification`, `net.scheme_set` and classifying annotations are\n  what it builds on, and it needs nothing of numeric.tn or\n  temporal.tn. A schema importing it reserves its root names beside the\n  kernel\'s, `net` among those ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3), so a schema imports it\n  when it wants these names, and only then. core.tn imports it with numeric.tn and\n  temporal.tn, for a schema that wants every bundled type.\n\n  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see meta.tn\'s\n  note.\n\n  References:\n    RFC 1123              Host name syntax (§2.1)\n    RFC 3492              Punycode\n    RFC 3986              URI syntax, and the IPv4address grammar\n    RFC 3987              Internationalized Resource Identifiers (ihost)\n    RFC 4291              IPv6 addressing architecture\n    RFC 4632              Classless Inter-domain Routing (CIDR)\n    RFC 5321, RFC 5322    Email address format\n    RFC 5890-5893         IDNA2008: definitions, protocol, code points, Bidi\n    RFC 5952              IPv6 address text representation\n    RFC 6838, RFC 6839    Media type specifications, structured suffixes\n    RFC 9110 §8.3.1       Media type parameters\n    RFC 9542              IEEE 802 parameters (EUI-48 format)\n    RFC 9562              UUID format\n  """\n!schema {\n  @doc:"The network constructors, each named by its path, `net.ipv4_type`."\n  net => !schema {\n    @doc:"""\n      Email constraint vocabulary. Instance is `email`. An address is\n      its text as written: a mailbox\'s local part is case-sensitive (RFC 5321\n      §2.4), so a form over the whole text could merge two mailboxes.\n      """\n    email_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:          = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5322"\n      normalization?: = NONE\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      IPv4 address constraint vocabulary. Instance is `ipv4`. The spec pin cites\n      RFC 3986 for its `IPv4address` production — the normative dotted-quad\n      grammar ([TSON-DATA] §5.5). `within` and `excluding` list IPv4 networks,\n      each a `cidr4`: the address must lie inside at least one `within` network\n      when the field is present, and inside no `excluding` network. `excluding`\n      carves holes out of `within`.\n      """\n    ipv4_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986"\n      within?:    [cidr4]\n      excluding?: [cidr4]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      IPv6 address constraint vocabulary. Instance is `ipv6`. `within` and\n      `excluding` list IPv6 networks, each a `cidr6`: the address must lie\n      inside at least one `within` network when the field is present, and inside\n      no `excluding` network.\n      """\n    ipv6_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4291"\n      within?:    [cidr6]\n      excluding?: [cidr6]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      IPv4 network constraint vocabulary. Instance is `cidr4`.\n      `min_prefix`/`max_prefix` narrow the prefix length within the family range\n      0-32; bounds outside that range are invalid at the schema level. `within`\n      lists IPv4 networks, each a `cidr4`: the value must be a subnet of at\n      least one. `excluding`: the value must not overlap any listed network —\n      overlap, not containment.\n      """\n    cidr4_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4632"\n      min_prefix?: numeric.count\n      max_prefix?: numeric.count\n      within?:     [cidr4]\n      excluding?:  [cidr4]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      IPv6 network constraint vocabulary. Instance is `cidr6`.\n      `min_prefix`/`max_prefix` narrow the prefix length within the family range\n      0-128. `within` and `excluding` list IPv6 networks, each a `cidr6`, with\n      the semantics of `cidr4_type`: subnet-of for `within`, non-overlap for\n      `excluding`.\n      """\n    cidr6_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4291"\n      min_prefix?: numeric.count\n      max_prefix?: numeric.count\n      within?:     [cidr6]\n      excluding?:  [cidr6]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"MAC address (EUI-48) constraint vocabulary. Instance is `mac`."\n    mac_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?: = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9542"\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Host name constraint vocabulary. Instance is `hostname`. A domain name as\n      a host names it: labels of ASCII letters, digits and hyphens (RFC 1123\n      §2.1), or internationalized labels judged by IDNA2008 (RFC 5890 to RFC\n      5893), written as U-labels or as their A-labels, in any mix. `A`-`Z` read\n      as `a`-`z`; nothing else is mapped, so uppercase beyond ASCII, a U-label\n      not in NFC and percent-encoding are not host names. A label is at most 63\n      octets and the name at most 253, counted in the A-label form; no label is\n      empty, none begins or ends with `-`, and there is no trailing `.`. The\n      last label does not begin with a digit, so a dotted-quad is never a host\n      name. The value is the name: every spelling of one name is one value, and\n      its canonical text has each internationalized label as its U-label.\n      `allow_idn` false admits only names with no internationalized label, in\n      either spelling.\n      """\n    hostname_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5890"\n      allow_idn?: boolean ~ true\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Host constraint vocabulary. Instance is `host`. RFC 3987\'s `ihost`\n      with a DNS name: a host name as `hostname_type` has it, an IPv4 address in\n      dotted-quad, or an IPv6 address, written with or without the brackets a\n      URI puts around one. No zone identifier. The value is the name or the\n      address, never both; a name compares by name, an address by its octets,\n      and an IPv4 address never equals an IPv6 one, IPv4-mapped included. The\n      canonical text is the member\'s: the name\'s U-labels, the dotted-quad, or\n      RFC 5952 text without brackets.\n      """\n    host_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?: = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987"\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      Media type constraint vocabulary. Instance is `media_type`. A media\n      type (RFC 6838) and its parameters (RFC 9110 §8.3.1): the type and subtype\n      are RFC 6838 §4.2\'s `restricted-name`, and each parameter is a token name,\n      `=` and a token or quoted-string value, after a `;` with optional\n      whitespace around it. Nothing beyond US-ASCII. A media range (`text/*`) is\n      not a media type. The value is the type, the subtype and a map of\n      parameters: the type, subtype and parameter names compare without ASCII\n      case, a quoted value equals its token form, parameter order carries no\n      meaning and a repeated name is a resolver error, and a parameter value\n      keeps its case except `charset`\'s, which folds. The canonical text is\n      lowercase, with `charset` folded and parameters sorted by name, each value\n      a token where it is one, joined by `;` without whitespace.\n      `allow_parameters` admits parameters, false by default. `types` lists the\n      top-level types admitted. `suffixes` lists RFC 6839 structured suffixes: a\n      suffix admits a subtype ending `+` and the suffix, and the subtype that is\n      the suffix itself. A refinement withdraws `allow_parameters` and shrinks\n      `types` and `suffixes`; it never widens them.\n      """\n    media_type_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:             = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6838"\n      allow_parameters?: boolean ~ false\n      types?:            [text]\n      suffixes?:         [text]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      URI constraint vocabulary per RFC 3986. Instances are `uri` and\n      `uri_reference`. The facets are `net.iri_type`\'s, over RFC 3986\'s\n      grammar: `schemes` lists the admitted schemes as `net.scheme_name` values,\n      which fold case; `allow_relative` admits a relative reference (§4.2)\n      beside a URI (§3), so its default is a URI-reference (§4.1);\n      `allow_fragment` admits a fragment (§3.5), and withdrawing it beside\n      `allow_relative` leaves an absolute-URI (§4.3). A URI is US-ASCII (§2),\n      and its text as written, as an IRI is.\n      """\n    uri_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:           = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986"\n      schemes?:        net.scheme_set\n      allow_relative?: boolean ~ true\n      allow_fragment?: boolean ~ true\n      normalization?:  = NONE\n    }\n\n    @doc:"UUID constraint vocabulary. Instance is `uuid`."\n    uuid_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {\n      spec?:    = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562"\n      version?: numeric.count\n    }\n  }\n\n  @doc:"Email address per RFC 5322."\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  email => !net.email_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    IPv4 address, dotted-quad per the RFC 3986 IPv4address production. Totally\n    ordered by numeric address value over a finite 32-bit space.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true\n  ipv4 => !net.ipv4_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    IPv6 address, RFC 4291 §2.2 text representation. Zone identifiers (RFC 4007,\n    `fe80::1%eth0`) are host-local and excluded from the contract. Totally\n    ordered by numeric address value over a finite 128-bit space. (TSON text:\n    contains colons, so quote it.)\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true\n  ipv6 => !net.ipv6_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    IPv4 network, address `/` prefix length 0-32 (RFC 4632). PARTIAL ordering is\n    by containment: a network precedes another when its address range is a\n    strict subset; CIDR blocks never partially overlap, so any two are nested or\n    disjoint, and disjoint networks are incomparable. (TSON text: contains `/`,\n    so quote it.)\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:PARTIAL @atom.bounded:false\n  cidr4 => !net.cidr4_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    IPv6 network, address `/` prefix length 0-128 (RFC 4291 §2.3). PARTIAL\n    ordering is by containment, as for cidr4. (TSON text: contains colons and\n    `/`, so quote it.)\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:PARTIAL @atom.bounded:false\n  cidr6 => !net.cidr6_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    MAC address, EUI-48: six hex octets, colon- or hyphen-separated (RFC 9542).\n    An opaque hardware identifier — like uuid, ordering is not semantically\n    meaningful. (TSON text: the colon form must be quoted; the hyphen form may\n    go unquoted.)\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  mac => !net.mac_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Internet host name, in either label form: `Example.COM` and `example.com`\n    are one value, and so are `xn--bcher-kva.example` and `bücher.example`,\n    whose canonical text is the second. See `net.hostname_type`.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  hostname => !net.hostname_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    A host: a `hostname`, an IPv4 address or an IPv6 address, one string-class\n    type, so `localhost`, `"127.0.0.1"`, `"::1"` and `"[::1]"` need no tag. See\n    `net.host_type`.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  host => !net.host_type {}\n\n  @doc:"""\n    A media type without parameters: `text/html`, `application/ld+json`. See\n    `net.media_type_type`.\n    """\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  media_type => !net.media_type_type {}\n\n  @doc:"A URI-reference (RFC 3986 §4.1): a URI or a relative reference."\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  uri_reference => !net.uri_type {}\n\n  @doc:"A URI (RFC 3986 §3): the scheme is required."\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  uri => !uri_reference ^ { allow_relative: false }\n\n  @doc:"128-bit identifier per RFC 9562."\n  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false\n  uuid => !net.uuid_type {}\n}\n';

/** `spec/m/core.tn`'s own header: `!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/core.tn?sha256=4e8d9c43cbbabf6a73548b819ac74e06e18859b573d05ce99988ae9d4d9a562e"` */
export const CORE_TN =
  '!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/core.tn?sha256=4e8d9c43cbbabf6a73548b819ac74e06e18859b573d05ce99988ae9d4d9a562e"\n!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/numeric.tn?sha256=dfa66c5d3775a7845776db737f07b16630824f2434f6587aface2f88b77bf0f2"\n!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/temporal.tn?sha256=fb4ada8f238de82821e7fcf512c21980a8b73c8df9260cdf295ba0157cc3971e"\n!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/net.tn?sha256=371fc20c8628b5461b56a7a28f2559a1cfde4eda718f72d60ceb1b5a007bac06"\n@doc:"""\n  TSON core — every bundled type in one import (2026 Revision 39 draft).\n\n  core.tn imports the three data-format libraries, numeric.tn, temporal.tn\n  and net.tn, each of which imports the kernel, meta.tn, alone. A schema\n  importing core therefore has the whole bundled vocabulary, each type under\n  one identity ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3): the common types at the roots of the\n  kernel and the libraries (`text`, `integer`, `bytes`, `enum`, `set`, `doc`,\n  `number`, `int32`, `date`, `datetime`, `uuid`, `uri`), and the machinery\n  under the maps those documents declare, by path (`meta.record`,\n  `meta.type_ref`, `unicode.text_type`, `numeric.decimal_type`,\n  `temporal.date_type`, `net.uuid_type`). It reserves the map names `meta`,\n  `atom`, `numeric`, `unicode`, `net`, `binary` and `temporal` and every root\n  name of those documents and of this one. A schema that wants only some of\n  the libraries imports those instead.\n\n  Its own root holds the author annotations, which describe a declaration or\n  a field for its readers and tools and never change what it admits:\n  `@deprecated`, `@title`, `@comment`, `@examples`, `@read_only` and\n  `@write_only`. It declares no namespace of its own.\n\n  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see meta.tn\'s\n  note.\n\n  References:\n    JSON Schema 2020-12   Meta-data and annotation vocabulary (`title`,\n                          `deprecated`, `readOnly`, `writeOnly`,\n                          `examples`, `$comment`)\n  """\n!schema {\n  @doc:"""\n    Marks a definition or field as deprecated. A bare presence marker like\n    `@atom.numeric`; the reason, where there is one, is `@doc`\'s to give.\n    """\n  deprecated => @meta.annotation void\n\n  @doc:"Short human-readable name for a definition or field."\n  title => @meta.annotation text\n\n  @doc:"""\n    A note for a schema\'s maintainers rather than its readers (JSON Schema\'s\n    `$comment`): where `@doc` is what a documentation tool shows, `@comment`\n    is what it leaves out.\n    """\n  comment => @meta.annotation text\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Example values for a definition or field, each the text a reader is shown:\n    conventionally the value written in TSON notation, so a record\'s example\n    is a string such as `"{ x: 1 }"`. Nothing parses or checks an example\n    against the type it illustrates; the annotation is documentation only.\n    """\n  examples => @meta.annotation [text]\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Marks a field an encoding\'s consumers may read but not write. A bare\n    presence marker like `@atom.numeric`: absence is the default, so there is no\n    false to write (JSON Schema\'s `readOnly: false` maps to no annotation).\n    """\n  read_only => @meta.annotation void\n\n  @doc:"""\n    Marks a field an encoding\'s consumers may write but not read. A bare\n    presence marker, on `@read_only`\'s terms.\n    """\n  write_only => @meta.annotation void\n}\n';

/** `spec/m/policy.tn`'s own header: `!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/policy.tn?sha256=e9cb5b9305ddec62f0e18e7ee7ed3d2a087d97a243a48b411daa11f5071a496f"` */
export const POLICY_TN =
  '!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/policy.tn?sha256=e9cb5b9305ddec62f0e18e7ee7ed3d2a087d97a243a48b411daa11f5071a496f"\n!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=c9b069b606f7cc8a043ebabe3fe91e4aaa35a9ffd814b68be0ba4bbe6d02bfb5"\n@doc:"""\n  TSON processor policy — what a processor admits and spends (2026 Revision 39\n  draft).\n\n  A processor\'s policy is a property of the deployment, never of a schema\n  ([TSON-DATA] §8.2): a schema that declared its own strictness would choose\n  its own check. This document declares the policy\'s vocabulary, so a\n  processor states the policy it judged under in one shape, and a deployment\n  that writes its policy down writes a data document of type\n  `policy.processor`. Such a document is named at the call site and never\n  resolved by identity, and no document may name, import or otherwise select\n  the policy it is judged under.\n\n  `policy.identifier_policy` and `policy.script_policy` are §8.2\'s two\n  policies, and `policy.limits` is §9.1\'s and [TSON-SCHEMA] §11.5\'s. A script\n  is named by its Unicode Script property value alias (UAX #24), `Latin`,\n  `Cyrillic`, `Han`.\n\n  It imports meta.tn alone, every field typed by the kernel\'s vocabulary or by\n  an entry of its own. Its entries are members of the schema map `policy`\n  nested in its root, each named by its path from the root, and a schema\n  whose import closure holds this document reserves that map name.\n  """\n!schema {\n  policy => !schema {\n    @doc:"UTS #39 §5.2\'s restriction levels, strictest first."\n    restriction_level => !enum [\n      ASCII_ONLY SINGLE_SCRIPT HIGHLY_RESTRICTIVE MODERATELY_RESTRICTIVE MINIMALLY_RESTRICTIVE UNRESTRICTED\n    ]\n\n    @doc:"""\n      [TSON-DATA] §8.2\'s token policy: the restriction level over each whole\n      token, and any script combinations admitted over the level (UTS #39\n      §5.2\'s own device for Latn+Jpan and its siblings), each entry in\n      `permitting` one such combination.\n      """\n    script_policy => { level: policy.restriction_level  permitting: [[text]] }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      [TSON-DATA] §8.2\'s identifier policy: the restriction level, whether it\n      applies to each segment of a name rather than to the whole of it,\n      whether no two names in one scope may share a UTS #39 skeleton, and any\n      script combinations admitted over the level, as `policy.script_policy`\n      states them.\n      """\n    identifier_policy => {\n      level: policy.restriction_level\n      per_segment: boolean\n      skeleton_distinctness: boolean\n      permitting: [[text]]\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      One resource limit: a count, a depth, or a size in code points, octets\n      or bytes, a positive `int64`, 1 to 2^63-1. A processor that holds a\n      limit in a narrower type than this range caps a larger value at the\n      widest it holds.\n      """\n    limit => !int64 ^ { min: 1 }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      [TSON-DATA] §9.1\'s resource limits and [TSON-SCHEMA] §11.5\'s: what a\n      processor will spend reading one document, and resolving one schema, as\n      against what it admits as a name. Every limit defaults to the spec\'s own\n      value, set at the tight end so that a document fitting it fits every\n      processor above, and a deployment writes only what it raises. A limit\n      refusal names the limit and the threshold, and is not a verdict on the\n      document.\n\n      The §9.1 limits bound a document, a schema document among them. Every\n      limit is a `policy.limit`.\n      The §11.5 limits bound the work resolution adds over a schema: the\n      schemas one header reaches through `!!import`, the\n      declarations in one schema map (synthetic and instantiation entries\n      included), the hops from a use site to a terminal entry, the length of\n      one type\'s supertype chain, the nested open synthetics closed for one\n      application, and the longest chain of waits one schema\'s resolution\n      runs, an entry waiting on the next being ready.\n      """\n    limits => {\n      max_depth?:                  policy.limit ~ 64\n      max_token_length?:           policy.limit ~ 1048576\n      max_text_length?:            policy.limit ~ 1048576\n      max_numeric_literal_length?: policy.limit ~ 4096\n      max_binary_size?:            policy.limit ~ 16777216\n      max_document_size?:          policy.limit ~ 16777216\n      max_elements?:               policy.limit ~ 1048576\n      max_entries?:                policy.limit ~ 1048576\n      max_fields?:                 policy.limit ~ 65536\n      max_annotations?:            policy.limit ~ 64\n      max_values?:                 policy.limit ~ 16777216\n      max_import_closure?:         policy.limit ~ 64\n      max_schema_entries?:         policy.limit ~ 65536\n      max_alias_chain?:            policy.limit ~ 64\n      max_supertype_chain?:        policy.limit ~ 64\n      max_materialisation_depth?:  policy.limit ~ 64\n      max_wait_depth?:             policy.limit ~ 64\n    }\n\n    @doc:"""\n      The policy a processor judges a document under: §8.2\'s identifier and\n      token policies and §9.1\'s limits. `unicode_data_version` is the UCD\n      release whose tables the §8.2 rules read, which a report states beside\n      the policy (§8.2) and a deployment\'s own policy document leaves out, the\n      tables being the processor\'s rather than the deployment\'s choice.\n      """\n    processor => {\n      identifier_policy: policy.identifier_policy\n      token_policy: policy.script_policy\n      unicode_data_version?: text\n      limits: policy.limits\n    }\n  }\n}\n';
